          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on published exercise guidance. General information, not medical advice.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#cost-by-type" class="btn-ghost">Cost by Type</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          The price of a treadmill stress test depends less on the treadmill than on what is added to
          it. A plain exercise ECG is one of the cheaper heart tests there is. Add an ultrasound of
          the heart before and after the walk and you have a stress echo; add a radioactive tracer
          and a camera and you have a nuclear stress test. Each step multiplies the bill, and who
          pays it — you, your insurer or Medicare — changes the number you actually see.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cost-by-type">Treadmill Stress Test Cost by Type</a></li>
            <li><a href="#sources-disagree">Why the Price Sources Disagree</a></li>
            <li><a href="#medicare">What Medicare Pays and What You Pay</a></li>
            <li><a href="#with-insurance">Cost With Private Insurance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cpt-codes">The Billing Codes to Ask About</a></li>
            <li><a href="#hospital-vs-clinic">Hospital vs. Outpatient Clinic or Cardiology Office</a></li>
            <li><a href="#get-a-price">How to Get a Real Price Before the Test</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cash-discounts">Paying Less Without Insurance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#home-treadmill">Why a Home Treadmill Is Not a Cheaper Substitute</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A basic treadmill stress test (an exercise ECG) typically costs a few hundred
            dollars if you pay cash, far less under Medicare, and considerably more once imaging is
            added.</strong> The figures below come from named sources, each checked in November 2026;
            your own price depends on where you have the test, your plan and your region.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Exercise ECG stress test, cash:</strong> $331 to $774 on the MDsave prepaid
            marketplace, with an MDsave national average of $491 (checked November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Exercise ECG stress test, Medicare rate:</strong> $73.48 nationally for CPT
            93015 under the 2026 Medicare Physician Fee Schedule, of which a patient who has met the
            Part B deductible pays 20%, about $14.70 (CMS data, checked November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Stress echocardiogram, cash:</strong> $330 to $1,868 on MDsave, national
            average $963 (checked November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Nuclear stress test, cash:</strong> $1,043 to $3,398 on MDsave, national
            average $2,230 (checked November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Hospital list prices:</strong> much higher. CostHelper's guide says uninsured
            patients will likely pay $1,000 to $5,000 for a stress test and its analysis (checked
            November 2026; the page carries no date of its own).</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One thing to settle before any of these numbers matter: the type of test is a medical
            decision, not a shopping one. Your doctor or cardiologist orders the version that
            answers their clinical question, and a cheaper test is only a saving if it is the right
            one. Treadmill Reviews USA reviews exercise equipment; this page is general cost
            information, not medical or insurance advice. Ask your doctor or cardiologist which test
            you need, and your insurer what you will owe for it. If you want to know what happens
            during the test itself, our guide to <a href="/treadmill-stress-test/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what a treadmill stress test shows</a> covers the session, the stages and how long it takes. Most of that session is the exercise itself: on the standard Bruce protocol, clinics aim for roughly 6 to 12 minutes on the belt, and our guide to <a href="/average-time-on-treadmill-stress-test-by-age/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">average stress test times by age</a> shows how that target shifts as you get older.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill Stress Test Cost by Type</h2>
<p>
            People searching for how much a treadmill stress test costs are usually quoted one of
            three different tests, and the gap between them is large. All three put you on a
            treadmill (or give you a medicine that makes the heart work as if you were exercising,
            if you cannot walk). What differs is how the heart is observed. In the plain version the
            ECG trace and blood pressure are the whole test. A stress echo adds ultrasound images of
            the heart at rest and straight after exercise. A nuclear stress test adds a small dose of
            radioactive tracer and a scanner that shows blood flow to the heart muscle.
          </p>
          <p>
            The table sets two kinds of price side by side. The MDsave columns are prepaid cash
            prices that participating providers list on the MDsave marketplace; they are real prices
            you can buy at, but only at those providers. The Medicare column is what Medicare's
            physician fee schedule sets as the total national payment in a doctor's office or
            independent clinic for 2026, before geographic adjustment. It is the closest thing
            there is to a public benchmark of what the work is worth to the biggest payer in the
            country.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Test</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">MDsave cash range</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">MDsave national average</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Medicare 2026 national amount (office)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Exercise ECG stress test (treadmill only)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$331-$774</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$491</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$73.48 (CPT 93015, global)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stress echocardiogram</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$330-$1,868</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$963</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$233.47 (CPT 93351, complete)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Nuclear stress test (myocardial perfusion SPECT)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,043-$3,398</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,230</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$427.87 for the imaging alone (CPT 78452), plus the stress test and tracer billed separately</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Sources: MDsave procedure pages for "Stress Test", "Stress Test Echo with or without
            Color Doppler" and "Nuclear Stress Test (Myocardial Perfusion Scan)"; CMS Physician Fee
            Schedule indicator file for 2026, total non-facility RVUs multiplied by the
            $33.4009 conversion factor that file lists for most clinicians. All checked November
            2026. MDsave's averages moved by a few dollars between checks in the same week, which is
            normal for a marketplace whose providers update their own listings.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. First, the imaging tests cost several times as much as the plain
            ECG test under every source, because they add expensive equipment, a sonographer or
            nuclear technologist, extra reading time and, for the nuclear test, the tracer itself.
            Second, even the cheapest cash prices sit well above what Medicare pays. That gap is
            the space in which negotiated insurance rates, self-pay discounts and hospital list
            prices all live, and it is why two people can be charged very different amounts for the
            same walk on the same treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sources-disagree" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why the Price Sources Disagree</h2>
<p>
            Look up how much is a treadmill stress test in three places and you can get three
            answers that do not overlap. That is not because some are wrong; they measure different
            things.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Marketplace cash prices</strong> such as MDsave's are prices a provider has
            agreed to accept up front, usually bundled so the facility and the reading physician
            are covered by one payment. They tend to be the lowest cash figures you will find,
            because providers who list there are competing on price.</li>
            <li><strong>"Estimated national averages"</strong> are comparison figures. MDsave shows
            one beside each of its own averages: $891 for the exercise test, $1,908 for the stress
            echo and $4,151 for the nuclear test (checked November 2026). The page does not
            explain how those estimates are built, so treat them as MDsave's view of a typical
            non-discounted price rather than as an independent benchmark.</li>
            <li><strong>Cost-guide publishers</strong> often quote hospital list prices or older
            data. CostHelper's stress test guide puts the uninsured cost at $1,000 to $5,000 and
            cites NewChoiceHealth for a national average of about $3,800, with a high of $10,900; it
            does not separate the three test types, and the page shows no date (checked November
            2026). Its figure is best read as the top of the market, not the middle.</li>
            <li><strong>Medicare amounts</strong> are administered prices, set by formula and
            published by CMS. They are not what a cash patient is charged, but many private
            insurers and self-pay discount schemes use Medicare as a reference point, so they help
            you judge whether a quote is reasonable.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical lesson is to compare like with like. A $500 quote for an exercise ECG at
            an independent cardiology office and a $3,000 bill for a nuclear stress test at a
            hospital are not two prices for the same thing. Confirm which test has been ordered,
            then compare quotes for that test, at the kind of facility you can actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="medicare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Medicare Pays and What You Pay</h2>
<p>
            Medicare Part B covers diagnostic non-laboratory tests, the category that includes EKGs
            and stress tests, when a provider orders them to find or treat a medical problem
            (Medicare.gov, checked November 2026). The ordering doctor has to document why the test
            is needed; a stress test requested simply for reassurance, with no symptoms or clinical
            reason, is the kind of service that can be refused as not medically necessary.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where the test happens changes what you owe. According to Medicare.gov, in a doctor's
            office or independent facility you pay 20% of the Medicare-approved amount after you
            meet the Part B deductible. In a hospital outpatient setting you also pay the hospital a
            copayment, which Medicare.gov says may be more than 20% of the approved amount, though
            usually it cannot exceed the Part A hospital stay deductible. The 2026 Part B deductible
            is $283, per CMS's 2026 premiums and deductibles fact sheet (checked November 2026).
          </p>
          <p>
            Put those together for a plain exercise ECG test in an office. Medicare's 2026 national
            amount for CPT 93015 is $73.48, so once the deductible is met the 20% share is about
            $14.70. If it is your first Part B service of the year, the deductible applies first,
            and you could pay the whole $73.48 yourself. For a stress echo (CPT 93351, $233.47) the
            20% share is about $46.69. These are national figures; Medicare adjusts payments by
            region, so a test in a high-cost city is paid somewhat more than one in a rural area.
          </p>
          <p>
            Supplemental (Medigap) coverage, Medicaid or a retiree plan can pick up some or all of
            that 20%. Medicare Advantage plans set their own cost-sharing and may require prior
            authorization or in-network providers, so the Original Medicare figures above do not
            apply to them. If you are on an Advantage plan, the plan's member services line or
            Evidence of Coverage document is where the real figure lives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="with-insurance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cost With Private Insurance</h2>
<p>
            For people with employer or marketplace insurance, the question is not the price of the
            test but how much of the plan's negotiated rate lands on you. Four things decide that.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Medical necessity.</strong> Insurers cover a stress test when it is ordered
            for a clinical reason, such as chest pain, breathlessness on exertion, an abnormal
            resting ECG or checking a known heart condition. Plans publish medical policies setting
            out when they consider each type of test appropriate, and the imaging versions usually
            face stricter rules than the plain ECG test.</li>
            <li><strong>Prior authorization.</strong> Many plans require approval before advanced
            cardiac imaging, which can include nuclear stress tests and sometimes stress echoes.
            Your doctor's office normally submits the request. Ask whether it has been approved
            before the appointment, not after.</li>
            <li><strong>Deductible.</strong> Until you have met your annual deductible, you usually
            pay the plan's full negotiated rate for diagnostic tests. On a high-deductible plan
            early in the year, that can mean paying most or all of the bill yourself.</li>
            <li><strong>Coinsurance or copay.</strong> After the deductible, you pay your plan's
            share, often a percentage of the negotiated rate for diagnostic tests and imaging, or a
            flat copay for some services. The plan pays the rest.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two further details decide whether that arithmetic holds. The facility and the reading
            physician should both be in your plan's network: a test at an in-network hospital can
            still produce a separate bill from the cardiologist who interprets it. And because a
            stress test is diagnostic rather than a screening service, it is generally not covered
            in full the way some preventive services are, so expect cost-sharing.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you have a health savings account or flexible spending account, a stress test your
            doctor orders is a medical expense of exactly the kind those accounts are meant for, and
            paying the deductible portion from one uses pre-tax money. Confirm with your plan
            administrator. Treadmills themselves are a different story; our guide to whether <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are HSA or FSA eligible</a> explains why the machine rarely qualifies when the test does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cpt-codes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Billing Codes to Ask About</h2>
<p>
            Every price lookup tool, insurer estimate and hospital price list is organized by
            billing code, so knowing the codes is the fastest way to get a comparable number. A
            plain treadmill stress test is billed under a family of four CPT codes. The descriptions
            below are summaries, and the Medicare amounts are 2026 national figures from the CMS
            Physician Fee Schedule indicator file (checked November 2026).
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Code</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it covers</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Medicare 2026 national amount</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">93015</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Whole exercise or drug stress test: supervision, tracing, interpretation and report (the "global" code)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$73.48</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">93016</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Physician supervision only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$20.71</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">93017</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tracing only (the equipment and technical part)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$39.08</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">93018</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Interpretation and report only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$13.69</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">93351</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Complete stress echocardiogram, including the ECG monitoring and supervision</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$233.47</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">78452</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Nuclear heart muscle imaging (SPECT), multiple studies; the stress test and tracer are billed separately</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$427.87</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Codes 93016, 93017 and 93018 add up to the same work as 93015 and exist because the
            parts are often done by different parties. When a hospital owns the treadmill and ECG
            equipment, it bills the technical part (93017) on its own claim, and the cardiologist
            bills supervision and interpretation (93016 and 93018) separately. That split is the
            reason a hospital-based test commonly produces two bills, and the reason a quote for
            "93015" from an office is not directly comparable with a hospital's quote for 93017
            alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask the office that orders your test which codes it expects to bill. If the test may
            become a stress echo or nuclear test, or may use a drug instead of the treadmill, ask
            about those codes too, plus any separate charges for the tracer or medication. With the
            codes in hand you can ask your insurer for an estimate and compare it against hospital
            price lists on equal terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hospital-vs-clinic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Hospital vs. Outpatient Clinic or Cardiology Office</h2>
<p>
            The same treadmill test can be done in a hospital outpatient department, an independent
            imaging or cardiology center, or a cardiologist's own office. The setting is often the
            single biggest driver of the price you see.
          </p>
          <p>
            Hospitals charge a facility fee for the use of their space, staff and equipment, on top
            of the physician's charge. Medicare.gov's own guidance reflects that: in a hospital
            outpatient setting you pay a copayment to the hospital as well, and it may be more than
            the 20% you would pay in an office. Private plans often set different cost-sharing for
            hospital-based and office-based services too, and hospital list prices, the starting
            point for an uninsured bill, tend to be the highest figures in the market.
          </p>
          <p>
            A cardiology practice that owns its own treadmill and ECG system bills the test as one
            global service, usually at a lower total. Freestanding imaging centers can offer lower
            prices for stress echo and nuclear tests for the same reason. The trade-offs are
            clinical, not financial: your cardiologist may want the test where previous results are
            on file, where a particular kind of imaging is available, or in a hospital because of a
            higher-risk history. Those reasons outrank the price difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you have a choice, it is reasonable to ask your doctor whether the test could be done
            in an office or independent center, and to compare what each would cost you. Doctors'
            offices field this question all the time, and many will say plainly when the setting
            matters and when it does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="get-a-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Get a Real Price Before the Test</h2>
<p>
            Average figures help you judge a quote. They are not a quote. The following steps get
            you a number for your own test, roughly in order of usefulness.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Get the codes and the place.</strong> Ask the ordering office which test,
            which CPT codes and which facility. Without those, nobody can give you a meaningful
            estimate.</li>
            <li><strong>If insured, ask your insurer.</strong> Most insurers offer a cost estimator
            in the member portal or by phone, which uses your plan's negotiated rate and how much of
            your deductible you have met. Ask about the facility and the interpreting physician
            separately, and whether prior authorization is needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the hospital's price transparency page.</strong> Since January 1,
            2021, CMS has required every US hospital to publish its standard charges in a
            machine-readable file covering all items and services, and in a consumer-friendly
            display of shoppable services (CMS hospital price transparency page, checked November
            2026). Search the hospital's name with "price transparency" or "standard charges" and
            look up the codes above. Hospitals commonly list a discounted cash price there as well
            as their gross charge.</li>
            <li><strong>If uninsured or self-pay, ask for a Good Faith Estimate.</strong> Under the
            No Surprises Act, if you are not using insurance, a provider must give you a good faith
            estimate when you schedule care at least three business days ahead or when you ask for
            one (CMS, checked November 2026). Schedule 10 or more business days out and the estimate
            is due within three business days.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the estimate.</strong> CMS says that if the final bill is at least $400
            more than the estimate from a provider, you can start the patient-provider dispute
            resolution process within 120 calendar days of the bill date, for a $25 fee (checked
            November 2026). Without a written estimate there is nothing to dispute.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Ask one more question at the booking stage: what happens to the price if the plan
            changes on the day. A test booked as a plain exercise ECG can become a drug-based or
            imaging test if, for example, you cannot walk long enough to reach the target heart
            rate. That is a clinical call made in the room, and it is better to know in advance how
            it would change the bill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cash-discounts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Paying Less Without Insurance</h2>
<p>
            If you are paying the full price yourself, the list price is the start of the
            conversation, not the end. Several routes reliably bring it down.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Ask for the self-pay or prompt-pay price.</strong> Many hospitals and
            practices have a discounted cash price below their gross charge, and some cut it further
            for payment in full at the time of service. Ask in those words; the discount is often
            not offered unless you do.</li>
            <li><strong>Compare prepaid marketplace prices.</strong> Platforms such as MDsave sell
            a bundled test in advance at a fixed price. MDsave says it refunds your payment in full
            if you end up not needing the procedure and do not receive care (checked November 2026).
            Check what the bundle includes and that the facility is one your doctor will accept
            results from.</li>
            <li><strong>Choose the setting, where the doctor agrees.</strong> An office or
            independent center is usually cheaper than a hospital outpatient department, for the
            reasons covered above.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask about financial assistance.</strong> Nonprofit hospitals are required
            to have a financial assistance policy, and many reduce or forgive bills for patients
            below set income levels. Ask for the application before the test, not after the bill
            arrives.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the Good Faith Estimate.</strong> As a self-pay patient you are entitled
            to one, and it is your protection if the final bill comes in well above it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What not to do is choose a cheaper test than the one ordered, or skip the test, purely
            on price. If cost is the obstacle, tell the doctor who ordered it. They may know a
            lower-cost site, be able to change the setting, or explain why the more expensive
            version is necessary in your case. That conversation is part of their job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why a Home Treadmill Is Not a Cheaper Substitute</h2>
<p>
            Because this site reviews treadmills, it is worth answering a question that sometimes
            follows a large quote: could you just walk on your own <a href="/best-treadmills-with-heart-rate-monitor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill with a heart rate
            monitor</a>? No. A clinical stress test is valuable because of what surrounds the treadmill:
            a multi-lead ECG read by trained staff, blood pressure checks at each stage, a
            supervising clinician and emergency equipment in the room. A home treadmill and a chest
            strap measure heart rate, which is one small part of that picture, and they cannot
            detect the ECG changes the test is looking for.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where home equipment does fit is afterwards. Many people are told after a normal test,
            or during cardiac rehabilitation, to exercise at a given intensity, and a treadmill makes
            that easy to control in any weather. If that is your situation, ask your doctor or cardiac
            rehab team for your target range first; our guide to <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a> explains how zones work and how to read them on a console. If you are pricing a machine
            for that purpose, the separate question of <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill costs</a> is covered tier by tier, and the <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best heart rate monitors for treadmill use</a> covers the straps and watches that read most reliably on a moving belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            To repeat the scope of this page: it explains costs, not care. Treadmill Reviews USA is
            an equipment review site and does not give medical advice. Whether you need a stress
            test, which version, and whether it is safe for you to exercise are questions for your
            doctor or cardiologist; what you will pay is a question for your insurer or the provider's
            billing office.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing a Stress Test</h2>
          <p>Most surprise bills for stress tests come from a handful of avoidable gaps between what was booked, what was done and what was asked beforehand.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing prices for different tests</h3>
              <p>A plain exercise ECG, a stress echo and a nuclear test can differ in price several times over. Confirm exactly which test and which CPT codes your doctor ordered before comparing quotes, or you will be comparing a $500 test with a $3,000 one.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the second bill</h3>
              <p>In a hospital, the facility and the interpreting cardiologist often bill separately. Ask for estimates from both, and check that both are in your plan's network, not only the hospital.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping prior authorization</h3>
              <p>Imaging stress tests often need approval from the insurer in advance. If nobody requests it, the claim can be denied and the bill lands on you. Ask the ordering office whether authorization is required and approved before the appointment.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying list price without asking</h3>
              <p>Self-pay patients who simply pay the bill often pay the gross charge. Ask for the discounted cash or prompt-pay price, request a Good Faith Estimate, and ask about financial assistance before the test.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A plain treadmill stress test is the cheapest version:</strong> $331 to $774 as a prepaid cash price on MDsave and $73.48 under Medicare's 2026 office
            rate, against $330 to $1,868 for a stress echo and $1,043 to $3,398 for a nuclear test
            on MDsave (all checked November 2026). Hospital list prices sit well above all of these.
          </p>
          <p>
            Get the CPT codes and the facility, ask your insurer or the provider for an estimate, and
            if you are paying yourself, ask for the self-pay price and a Good Faith Estimate. Which
            test you need is your doctor's or cardiologist's call, not a price decision. For what
            happens during the test itself, read our guide to <a href="/treadmill-stress-test/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">what a treadmill stress test shows</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

