        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a treadmill stress test cost without insurance?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A plain exercise ECG stress test was listed at $331 to $774 as a prepaid cash price on MDsave, with a national average of $491 (checked November 2026). Hospital list prices run much higher; CostHelper puts the uninsured cost of a stress test at $1,000 to $5,000. Ask any provider for its self-pay price and a Good Faith Estimate.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a treadmill stress test with Medicare?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Medicare's 2026 national amount for a complete exercise stress test (CPT 93015) in a doctor's office is $73.48. Once you have met the $283 Part B deductible, you pay 20%, about $14.70. In a hospital outpatient department you also owe the hospital a copayment, so the total is higher.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a stress test cost with insurance?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on your plan's negotiated rate, how much of your deductible is left and your coinsurance or copay. Before the deductible is met you may pay the full negotiated rate; afterwards you pay your share. Your insurer's cost estimator, given the CPT codes and the facility, is the reliable way to find out.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is a nuclear stress test so much more expensive?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It adds a radioactive tracer, a nuclear camera, a nuclear technologist and extra reading time to the treadmill test. On MDsave it was listed at $1,043 to $3,398 against $331 to $774 for the plain exercise test (checked November 2026). Under Medicare the imaging alone (CPT 78452) is $427.87 in 2026, before the stress test and tracer.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the CPT code for a treadmill stress test?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              CPT 93015 is the global code for a cardiovascular stress test, covering supervision, tracing, interpretation and report. When the work is split, 93016 is supervision only, 93017 is the tracing (technical part) and 93018 is interpretation and report. A stress echo is usually billed as 93351 and nuclear imaging as 78452.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does insurance cover a treadmill stress test?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, when your doctor orders it for a clinical reason such as chest pain or a known heart condition. Insurers may refuse tests they consider not medically necessary, and imaging versions often need prior authorization. Coverage does not mean free: deductibles, coinsurance and out-of-network charges still apply.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a stress test cheaper at a hospital or a clinic?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually at a clinic or cardiology office, because hospitals add a facility fee and Medicare patients owe a hospital outpatient copayment on top of the physician's share. Your doctor may still prefer a hospital for clinical reasons, which take priority over the price difference.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
